A||||4||btoth||Overfilled transmission??||||4||
Z||000000||btoth||10-28-2001||06:23 PM||||Checked my transmission fluid level today.  (First time sinse I bought the car, I think.)  Checked it after it was properly warmed up.  The fluid level is a good half-inch over the "full hot" line.  I would assume that this isn't a good thing.  What problems could this cause?  (My trans does shift pretty hard from N to D.)<P>Btw: '86 GT, auto.<P>Thanks.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Mach10||10-28-2001||06:25 PM||||My experience is that when things heat up, it will start dumping fluid (vomiting, actually) out the breather pipe... Other than that, I don't believe it does all that much... But that probably depends on how the valve bodies are set up... ||||reg||6
Z||000002||btoth||10-29-2001||07:41 AM||||*||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-29-2001||12:18 PM||||You checked it hot, and running, in park? Has to be running when checked or it will read over full.<P>If it's a little over filled it probably won't hurt anything. Sounds like you are about a pint over. Often happens when someons gets to add and dumps in a quart... The stick says add 1 pint but most people don't read it.<P>Ideally you'd want to pull that down but it's probably not hurting it any. If you think it's more than 1 pint over, then definatly pull it down.<P>If needed you can pull it down some with a section of plastic tubing and almost any good size squeeze bulb or turkey baster easy enough.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||10-31-2001||08:58 AM||||If fluid is too high in an auto tranny you can get foaming in the trans which causes circulation problems.  It's not healthy but won't cause it to self distruct anytime soon.||||reg||1
